WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.927
Permission-sensitive code should be tested
in both success and failure conditions.

2
00:00:05.007 --> 00:00:08.943
Tests should cover expected permissions,
denied access, read-only files,

3
00:00:09.023 --> 00:00:12.282
missing directories, shared directories,
cleanup behavior,

4
00:00:12.362 --> 00:00:15.129
and cases where creation or permission
changes fail.

5
00:00:15.209 --> 00:00:18.714
These are ordinary operational conditions,
not exotic edge cases.

6
00:00:18.794 --> 00:00:23.098
Compiler warnings and static analysis can
help identify unchecked return values,

7
00:00:23.178 --> 00:00:26.560
risky file-control paths, unsafe string
handling around paths,

8
00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.100
and cleanup branches that skip important
work.

9
00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:31.763
Tools do not understand every permission
policy,

10
00:00:31.843 --> 00:00:35.962
but they are useful signals when code
ignores results from security-sensitive

11
00:00:36.042 --> 00:00:36.719
operations.

12
00:00:36.799 --> 00:00:39.258
Review should trace the entire file
lifecycle.

13
00:00:39.338 --> 00:00:40.691
Where is the file created?

14
00:00:40.771 --> 00:00:42.124
Which access mode is used?

15
00:00:42.204 --> 00:00:43.926
Which permissions are expected?

16
00:00:44.006 --> 00:00:45.543
Which owner or group matters?

17
00:00:45.623 --> 00:00:46.730
Is metadata checked?

18
00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:48.286
What happens during writes?

19
00:00:48.366 --> 00:00:49.657
What happens on failure?

20
00:00:49.737 --> 00:00:50.967
How is cleanup handled?

21
00:00:51.047 --> 00:00:55.413
A review that follows the lifecycle is
much stronger than one that inspects isolated

22
00:00:55.493 --> 00:00:55.862
calls.

23
00:00:55.942 --> 00:00:59.324
Deployment documentation should state
required users, groups,

24
00:00:59.404 --> 00:01:02.294
directories, permissions, and platform
assumptions.

25
00:01:02.374 --> 00:01:04.157
Code and operations have to agree.

26
00:01:04.237 --> 00:01:08.111
If the application expects a service
account, a private report directory,

27
00:01:08.191 --> 00:01:11.265
a shared group, or a particular temporary
location policy,

28
00:01:11.345 --> 00:01:15.527
that expectation should be visible to the
people installing and maintaining the

29
00:01:15.607 --> 00:01:16.037
system.

30
00:01:16.117 --> 00:01:18.208
Operational validation catches drift.

31
00:01:18.288 --> 00:01:21.793
Permissions can change after installation,
updates, migrations,

32
00:01:21.873 --> 00:01:25.255
manual maintenance, backup restoration, or
incident response.

33
00:01:25.335 --> 00:01:27.672
Periodic checks, deployment health checks,

34
00:01:27.752 --> 00:01:32.117
or administrative review can detect when
the real environment no longer matches the

35
00:01:32.197 --> 00:01:33.673
intended permission model.

36
00:01:33.753 --> 00:01:38.119
Good permission security is coordinated
across code, configuration, deployment,

37
00:01:38.199 --> 00:01:39.060
and operations.
